Copy-engine test backend
========================

This out-of-tree module is a CPU stand-in for the mechanism copy-engine
provider. It uses a vmalloc window and memcpy in a workqueue. It exercises
CE plan validation, completion classification, ownership and quarantine;
it does not establish GPU DMA execution or hardware performance.

Build against a matching xdev_ext kernel with CONFIG_XDEV_EXT_MECH_CE=y.
The Makefile produces xdev_ext_ce_sim.ko. The integration guest harness
builds and loads this module inside its isolated guest.

Existing module parameters select the window and segment sizes, delayed
completion (delay_us), and fail_mode=none, pre_submit or ambiguous.
Ambiguous completion deliberately retains resources; use a fresh guest for
that failure case. See the repository testing guide for evidence checks.

CE chains a and d copy every batch out to the window and back into the same
host frames, so comparing those frames passes even if no copy ran. The
control tool therefore fills the window with 0xc3 before the session and
checks it afterwards (XDEV_EXT_MECH_CE_WINDOW line): at least one slot must
hold copied data, no data piece may still be 0xc3, chain d's slots must
match the frame pattern, and nothing may be written past the used slots.
The read()/write() interface above is what the tool uses here. A D2H copy
that was skipped while the H2D copy ran is still not detected: the frames
already hold those bytes.

Supplemental D2H diagnostic
--------------------------

`poison_after_h2d=1` is default-off and captured per submit. It inverts only
Host payload bytes after every H2D read has finished and before completion
can submit D2H. With device=P and Host=~P, D2H must restore Host=P. This option
is restricted to isolated serial diagnostics with non-overlapping Host
payloads; it is not for shared frames, fan-out or concurrent sessions.

`fail_mode=skip_h2d` and `skip_d2h` preserve success and the nominal full byte
count while omitting that direction's data copy. They test data observation,
not backend completion errors. Original campaigns and defaults are unchanged.
A single group is required to avoid repeated inversion cancelling itself.

Host regression compiles the real copy function and bounds helper under
GCC ASan/UBSan, compares independent expected buffers including all guards,
and verifies late invalid SGL entries cannot copy any legal prefix. Generated
mutants remove inversion, move it before H2D, or move full-request validation
into the copy loop; only explicit assertion failures count as detections.

Run `python3 tests/support/ce_sim/copy_contract.py --mutations --output build/ce-copy`.
The existing CE roundtrip Python entry also runs this regression through the
source view. Host success does not certify a kernel module build, KVM runtime,
actual GPU DMA or performance. The `--ce-sim` KVM harness also runs a separate single-group diagnostic
before the ordinary campaign. Each a/d normal/skip_d2h case loads a fresh
module, captures raw CLI records and parameters, requires exactly two CE
commands, detaches the plan, drains owners, and unloads before a summary.
The host console validator requires all four raw case blocks; a summary
alone is rejected. Chain a compares its independent NVMe source/sink;
chain d reports full Host payload and guard mismatch counts. A skipped D2H
must leave exactly 8192 wrong payload bytes and no wrong guard bytes.

`xdev_ext_mech_ce_d2h_test.py` executes the actual POSIX guest script under
sh and BusyBox sh with OS/CLI boundaries represented by host fixtures,
and attacks missing, duplicate and inconsistent raw evidence. These are
host fixtures, not a claim that a KVM guest or module was actually run.
